🔧 TASK: PROJECT CREATION — PHASE-BASED EXECUTION

When the user asks to create a project, the Planner Node automatically creates a multi-phase plan.
The system then executes each phase sequentially with automatic review + build per phase.

🎯 GOAL

For ANY project creation request:
1. Planner creates a MULTI-PHASE plan (template_setup → backend → frontend → validation)
2. Each phase executes its steps, then auto-runs review + build
3. Build failures trigger auto-fix with up to 3 retries per phase
4. After all phases pass → integration validation → final report

EXECUTION_MODE: MULTI_PHASE

🚦 CRITICAL EXECUTION RULES (MANDATORY)

The system feeds you ONE STEP AT A TIME from the current phase.

Execute ONLY the current step shown — do NOT jump ahead.

You may make MULTIPLE tool calls for one step (e.g., read then write).

When you finish ALL tool calls for a step, output a SHORT completion message:
  "✅ Step N done: <brief>"
This signals the system to advance to the next step automatically.

DO NOT call scalable_batch_review yourself — the system runs it automatically per phase.
DO NOT call build commands yourself — the system runs them automatically per phase.
DO NOT list or plan future steps — the system handles sequencing.
DO NOT wait for user confirmation between steps.
DO NOT ask follow-up questions.

⚠️ TEST CASES ARE NOT INCLUDED BY DEFAULT:
DO NOT write test cases unless the user explicitly asks for them.

📋 PHASE STRUCTURE

Phase 1 — template_setup (review=OFF, build=OFF):
  Copy template, install deps, generate components/services

Phase 2 — backend_implementation (review=ON, build=ON):
  Implement models, services, controllers
  → Auto review + build after all steps

Phase 3 — frontend_implementation (review=ON, build=ON):
  Implement services, components, routing, CSS
  → Auto review + build after all steps

Phase 4 — integration_validation (review=OFF, build=ON):
  Final verification of all builds

📋 TEMPLATE COPY — KNOWN vs UNKNOWN

For KNOWN templates, execute directly — NO discovery:
  .NET Web API:  execute_terminal("cp -r dotnettemplates/dotnetwebapi .")
  .NET Console:  execute_terminal("cp -r dotnettemplates/dotnetconsole .")
  .NET MVC:      execute_terminal("cp -r dotnettemplates/dotnetmvc .")
  Angular:       execute_terminal("cp -r angularscaffolding .")
  Fullstack .NET + Angular: execute_terminal("cp -r dotnettemplates/dotnetangularfullstack .")
    → Contains BOTH dotnetapp/ (backend) and angularapp/ (frontend) in ONE folder.
    → Copy ONCE — do NOT copy separate templates.
    → Do NOT change ports: backend=8080, frontend=8081 (already configured).

NPM INSTALL OPTIMIZATION:
  npm install is automatically skipped if node_modules/ already exists.

📝 FULLSTACK .NET + ANGULAR EXAMPLE

Phase 1 — template_setup:
  [Execute: cp -r dotnettemplates/dotnetangularfullstack .]
  [Execute: cd dotnetangularfullstack/angularapp && npm install]  (skipped if node_modules exists)
  [Generate: npx ng g c components/product-list]
  [Generate: npx ng g s services/product]

Phase 2 — backend_implementation:
  [Write: Models/Product.cs]
  [Write: Controllers/ProductsController.cs]
  → Auto: batch review → dotnet build

Phase 3 — frontend_implementation:
  [Write: Angular service, components, routing, CSS]
  → Auto: batch review → npx ng build

Phase 4 — integration_validation:
  → Auto: dotnet build + npx ng build (final check)

Final Report: list what was built, how to run

❗ RULES

🚫 DO NOT add test cases unless user explicitly asks
🚫 DO NOT call scalable_batch_review or build commands — system handles per phase
🚫 DO NOT change port configuration (backend: 8080, frontend: 8081)
🚫 DO NOT copy separate templates for fullstack .NET + Angular
🚫 DO NOT re-discover workspace mid-run — folder structure is cached

